% Goal

Choose and format pop-up cards for `user_stories/story__while_the_mix_plays_pop_ups_show_music_facts_and_my_commentary.md`.
This module has no network, audio, Mixxx or GUI dependency. `hands/popup_overlay.py`
serves the cards live and the video builders burn them in.

% Requirements

1. A `Card` has `kind` "fact" (written by the agent), "quote" (an author's own words) or "distilled" (a distillation the author kept). Fact cards have `initials` None; quote and distilled cards always carry the author's initials (Ernest: "EY").
2. `load_facts` reads the tracked JSON facts file (`brain/popup_facts.json`): a list of objects with `id`, `text`, `artists` (empty list = general), optional `titles`, `source` (required, non-empty: where the fact can be checked) and `verified` (bool, default false). A fact without a source is rejected with ValueError, so no fact airs without a stated source.
3. `load_commentary(root)` reads `root/<author-slug>/`:
   - Author: `author.json` gives `name` and `initials`. Without it, initials come from the slug's words ("ernest-yeung" -> "EY").
   - Files: `*.md`, skipping files whose name starts with `_` and `README.md`.
   - Front matter: an optional block between `---` lines gives `title` and `tags` (`[a, b]` or `a, b`).
   - Body: if a file has blockquote lines (`> `), each run of them is one pull quote and nothing else in the file is used. Otherwise each paragraph is a passage. Headings, lists and code are skipped.
   - Each pull quote or passage becomes one thread (a list of quote cards) via `snippets`, numbered with `part=(i, n)` when n > 1.
   - `distilled.md` is special: each `- ` bullet is one distilled card (its own thread) tagged with that file's tags.
   - Card ids are stable: author slug, file name and position.
4. `snippets(text, max_chars)` splits on sentence ends and joins consecutive sentences while they fit. A sentence longer than `max_chars` is split at word boundaries. Order is preserved, no text is lost, and nothing is empty.
5. `is_relevant(card, artist, title)` compares case-insensitively and ignores punctuation, accents (Mýa = Mya) and a leading "the":
   - A fact is relevant when one of its artists appears in the playing artist string (features such as "Mýa; Jadakiss" or "Joe Featuring Mariah Carey & Nas" count each name), and, if it lists titles, the title matches one.
   - Commentary is relevant when one of its tags names the playing artist.
   - General facts and untagged commentary are never "relevant"; they are fallbacks.
6. `Scheduler.next_card(artist, title)`:
   - Mix: every `commentary_every`-th card is commentary when any exists; the rest are facts. If the chosen kind has nothing, use the other.
   - Threads: a started thread continues in order on the next commentary turn until it ends.
   - Preference: relevant first, then general facts / untagged threads, then any.
   - No repeats: never repeat a card while another eligible one hasn't been shown; when all have been shown, the rotation restarts.
   - `verified_only` drops unverified facts.
   - Same inputs and seed give the same sequence.
7. `Scheduler.tick(now, artist, title)` returns a new card when at least `interval_s` has passed since the last one (or on the first call), else None. `interval_s` must be >= `show_s` (ValueError otherwise).
8. `parse_cue_sheet` reads a Mixxx `.cue` (TRACK / TITLE / PERFORMER / INDEX 01 mm:ss:ff at 75 frames per second). Each track ends where the next starts; the last ends at `duration_s` when given, else 600 s later.
9. `timeline(segments, scheduler)` gives `{start, end, artist, title, card}` items. The first card in a segment starts `first_offset_s` in; then every `interval_s`. Each card shows `show_s`, lies entirely inside its segment (ending >= 1 s before the segment ends), and never overlaps another.
10. `card_lines` wraps the card text to `width` characters and wraps a quote in curly quotation marks. A distilled card gets the line "in brief". A card with `part` gets "i/n". Commentary ends with "— <initials>" unless `attribution=False`, which only a display that shows the initials as a separate badge may use. Facts get no attribution line.
11. `drawtext_filters(items, out_dir, fontfile)` writes one text file per item into `out_dir` (no escaping problems) and returns a comma-joined ffmpeg drawtext chain enabled only between each item's start and end, with a semi-transparent box.
12. CLI `python -m brain.popups preview --artist A --title T [--count N]` prints the next N cards. `python -m brain.popups timeline --cue FILE [--duration S] [--out FILE.json]` writes the video timeline.

% Verification

- `uv run python -m unittest tests/test_popups.py` (temporary directories only).
- Cover:
  - initials rules (facts never have EY; commentary always does; other authors get their own initials);
  - template and README skipping, front matter tags, pull-quote precedence, distilled bullets;
  - snippet bounds;
  - relevance with features and accents;
  - cadence, threads and no-repeat rotation;
  - verified_only;
  - interval < show rejected; fact without source rejected;
  - cue parsing;
  - timeline bounds and no overlap;
  - card_lines attribution;
  - drawtext output.
